      "role": "user",
      "content": "I am currently investigating the kinetic implications of stochastic molecular collisions on the structural integrity of the lecithin-based interfacial film in a high-acid (pH < 3.5) low-fat emulsion. \n\nWe are observing a phenomenon where the surfactant layer appears to undergo sudden, localized disruptions that lead to rapid droplet coalescence, seemingly independent of the average shear rate. Can you provide a technical analysis of how the increased proton concentration and the resulting reduction in electrostatic repulsion might exacerbate the impact of these stochastic collisions? Specifically, I am looking for an explanation of how the mechanical agitation facilitates \"breakthrough\" events where droplets bypass the energy barrier due to localized fluctuations in the interfacial film density.",
